  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 PM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\n*** Tonight through Friday Night *** \n\nWarm and dry conditions will continue through the end of the work \nweek as surface high pressure remain over the region. Relative \nhumidity is expected to drop again today as dry air above the surface \nis being mixed down. Tonight temperatures will cool into the low 50s \nacross the forecast area. Winds will begin to shift to a SE wind as \nthe center of the high pressure continues east. Slightly warmer \ntemperatures for Friday with highs in the low to mid 80s, and \nnighttime temperatures in the upper 50s and low 60s. \n\n\n*** Saturday through Mid Next Week ***\n\nHurricane Isaias is expected to make landfall in the Gulf coast \nFriday evening/night. This system is expected to take north through \nMississippi and Alabama, and eventually making its way up to our \nregion this weekend. \n\nAs the upper ridge continues east, troughing to the west will phase \ntogether with the low pressure system. Much needed moisture will be \nbrought into the Ohio Valley from the Gulf as the remnants of Isaias \ntrack north. The associated rain shield is expected to move in to \nthe southern KY counties by mid-day Saturday. The main threats with \nthe system will be heavy rainfall and gusty winds with onset. Given \nhow dry the area has been, this rainfall should be more beneficial \nand poses a low flooding threat. OPF values are ranging from 0.5\" to \n1.5\" with the high amounts through central KY. Localized areas of \n3\"+ are still possible (20% chance), which will become more certain \npost landfall. This highest wind gusts are expected late Saturday \nevening. Southern KY could see wind gusts 25-35 mph, while the rest \nof the forecast area would be lower as the system weakens. Chances \nfor severe weather remain low as instability will be at a minimal \nwith onset. Guidance is consist with little to no surface based CAPE \nacross the area limited to available energy for convection to form.\n\nSunday into Monday, the remainder of the system will phase in with \nthe jet stream and push out to the east. By Monday, synoptic ridging \nwill begin to build back into the region. This will bring drier \nconditions and clearer skies back for the start of the week. Maximum \ntemperatures are expected to be in the low to mid 80s and nighttime \nlows in the upper 50s and low 60s.",
